   "beats": [
     {
       "id": "beat_1_lumped_abstraction",
       "title": "Lumped Abstraction",
       "narration": "We begin by adopting the lumped circuit abstraction, which treats components as localized entities where internal electromagnetic wave propagation is negligible compared to signal travel times. This assumption allows us to model circuits as interconnected nodes and branches rather than distributed fields. Imagine a simple battery connected to a resistor through short wires; the voltage appears instantaneously across the resistor because the physical dimensions are tiny relative to the signal wavelength. This abstraction holds when the circuit size is much smaller than the signal's wavelength, ensuring that voltage and current are well-defined at each node. We will consistently use this model throughout our analysis.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a simplified circuit with a battery, resistor, and capacitor connected by thin lines. The diagram includes a callout box highlighting:",
