       "claimIds": ["mit-circuits"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a lumped circuit model with a battery, resistor, and capacitor connected by thin lines. The callout box highlights the assumption that dimensions are small relative to signal wavelength, ensuring well-defined voltages and currents at each node.'"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_2_reference_directions",
       "title": "Reference Directions",
       "narration": "Next, we establish consistent reference polarities and current directions for every element. This systematic approach prevents sign errors and ensures our equations reflect physical reality. For example, if we assign the positive terminal of a battery to the top node and define current flowing clockwise through the resistor, we must maintain these conventions throughout the analysis. The direction we choose is arbitrary, but consistency is critical. When we later write equations, a positive voltage drop across a resistor means current flows from the higher to lower potential, while a negative value indicates the actual direction opposes our assumption. This disciplined labeling simplifies solving the resulting algebraic system.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a simple series circuit with a battery, resistor, and capacitor. Each element has labeled voltage polarities (positive/negative) and a",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuit-notes"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a series circuit with a battery, resistor, and capacitor. Each element has labeled voltage polarities and current arrows. The narrator explains that these directions are arbitrary but must be kept consistent throughout the analysis to avoid sign errors and ensure physical accuracy.'"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_3_kirchhoff_laws",
       "title": "Kirchhoff Laws",
       "narration": "With reference directions set, we apply Kirchhoff’s current and voltage laws to constrain the circuit. Kirchhoff’s current law states that the sum of currents entering any node equals zero, reflecting charge conservation. Kirchhoff’s voltage law requires that the sum of voltage drops around any closed loop equals zero, reflecting energy conservation. For our series circuit, we write one KCL equation at the top node and one KVL equation around the loop. These constraints reduce the number of unknowns and provide the structural framework for solving the circuit. We then combine these with element laws to form a complete set of equations that uniquely determine all voltages and currents.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a simple series circuit with a battery, resistor, and capacitor. Each element has labeled voltage polarities and current arrows. The K",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuits"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a series circuit with a battery, resistor, and capacitor. The KCL equation is shown at the top node, and the KVL equation is shown around the loop. The narrator explains that these constraints reduce unknowns and provide the structural framework for solving the circuit.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_4_resistor_capacitor_relations",
       "title": "Resistor and Capacitor Constitutive Relations",
       "narration": "We now introduce the constitutive relations that describe how resistors and capacitors behave. A resistor obeys Ohm’s law, where voltage drop equals resistance times current. A capacitor relates current to the rate of voltage change, storing electric-field energy as charge accumulates on its plates. These relations are fundamental because they connect electrical quantities to physical mechanisms. For example, when current flows into a capacitor, voltage rises proportionally to the accumulated charge. We use these laws alongside Kirchhoff constraints to build the complete mathematical model of the circuit, ensuring every element’s behavior is accurately represented.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor with labeled voltage and current. The resistor has a voltage drop arrow, and the capacitor has a voltage rise","plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuits"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor with labeled voltage and current. The resistor has a voltage drop arrow, and the capacitor has a voltage rise arrow. The narrator explains that these relations connect electrical quantities to physical mechanisms, ensuring every element’s behavior is accurately represented.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_5_rc_time_constant",
       "title": "RC Time Constant",
       "narration": "When we combine a resistor and capacitor in series, the circuit exhibits a characteristic time constant, tau, defined as resistance times capacitance. This value determines how quickly the circuit responds to changes. For example, if tau equals one second, the capacitor voltage will reach approximately 63 percent of its final value after one second. The time constant emerges naturally from solving the differential equation that governs the circuit. It represents the intrinsic speed of the system, balancing the resistor’s opposition to current flow with the capacitor’s ability to store charge. Understanding tau allows us to predict response times without solving the full equation every time.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuits"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage rising over time. The narrator explains that tau determines how quickly the circuit responds to changes, representing the intrinsic speed of the system.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_6_charging_discharging",
       "title": "Charging and Discharging",
       "narration": "We now analyze how the capacitor charges and discharges when connected to a voltage source. During charging, current flows from the source through the resistor into the capacitor, causing voltage to rise exponentially. The differential equation governing this process yields a solution where voltage approaches the source value asymptotically. Discharging occurs when the source is removed and the capacitor releases its stored energy through the resistor. The voltage decays exponentially, following the same time constant but in reverse. These behaviors demonstrate how energy transfers between the electric field and the resistor, with the resistor dissipating energy as heat while the capacitor temporarily stores it.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuit-notes"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage rising and falling over time. The narrator explains that charging causes voltage to rise exponentially, while discharging causes it to decay, demonstrating energy transfer between the electric field and the resistor.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_7_transient_steady_state",
       "title": "Transient versus Steady State",
       "narration": "We distinguish between transient and steady-state behavior to understand long-term circuit operation. The transient phase occurs immediately after a change, characterized by exponential charging or discharging governed by the time constant. The steady state emerges after several time constants, when the capacitor voltage stabilizes at the source value. Initial capacitor voltage is continuous unless an impulse supplies unbounded current, ensuring no sudden jumps in stored energy. Final steady-state behavior can be checked independently by replacing the capacitor with a short circuit, which simplifies the analysis. This separation allows us to verify solutions and predict long-term performance without solving the full differential equation.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuit-notes"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage rising and falling over time. The narrator explains that the transient phase occurs immediately after a change, while the steady state emerges after several time constants, ensuring no sudden jumps in stored energy.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_8_low_pass_filter",
       "title": "Low-Pass Filtering",
       "narration": "We now examine how the RC circuit functions as a low-pass filter, attenuating high-frequency signals while passing low-frequency ones. When the input voltage changes slowly, the capacitor has time to charge fully, allowing the output to follow the input closely. When the input changes rapidly, the capacitor cannot charge quickly enough, so the output voltage lags and attenuates. This frequency-dependent behavior arises because the capacitor’s impedance decreases with increasing frequency. The circuit thus acts as a smooth filter, removing rapid fluctuations while preserving slow variations. This property is essential in audio and signal processing applications where clean, stable signals are required.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuits"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage rising and falling over time. The narrator explains that the circuit functions as a low-pass filter, attenuating high-frequency signals while passing low-frequency ones, removing rapid fluctuations while preserving slow variations.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_9_cutoff_frequency",
       "title": "Cutoff Frequency",
       "narration": "We define the cutoff frequency, omega_c, as the point where the output voltage drops to 35 percent of its input value. This frequency is inversely proportional to the time constant, calculated as one divided by tau. Signals with frequencies below omega_c pass through with minimal attenuation, while signals above omega_c are significantly reduced. The cutoff frequency provides a clear boundary for the circuit’s frequency response, indicating the highest frequency it can effectively transmit. By adjusting the resistor or capacitor values, we can shift this boundary to filter different signal ranges. This parameter is crucial for designing filters that meet specific frequency requirements.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuit-notes"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage rising and falling over time. The narrator explains that the cutoff frequency is the point where output voltage drops to 35 percent of input, providing a clear boundary for the circuit’s frequency response.'}"
     }
   },
     {
       "id": "beat_10_energy_step_response",
       "title": "Energy and Step-Response Calculation",
       "narration": "Finally, we calculate the complete step response and verify energy conservation. When a voltage step is applied, the capacitor charges exponentially, and the resistor dissipates energy as heat. The total energy stored in the capacitor equals the energy supplied by the source minus the energy dissipated in the resistor. Limiting cases at zero time, long time, zero frequency, and high frequency provide useful error checks. Time-domain step response and frequency-domain filtering are complementary descriptions of the same linear circuit behavior. By solving the differential equation and verifying these checks, we ensure our solution is accurate and physically meaningful.",
       "visualDirection": "A clear, editable educational diagram showing a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage",
       "plannedSeconds": 65,
       "claimIds": ["mit-circuit-notes"],"accessibility": {
       "describedInNarration": true,
       "audioDescriptionCue": "Narrator describes the diagram: 'This diagram shows a resistor and capacitor in series with a voltage source. The time constant tau is labeled, and a graph shows the capacitor voltage rising and falling over time. The narrator explains that the step response calculation verifies energy conservation, with limiting cases providing error checks and complementary frequency-domain descriptions.'}"
     }
   }
   ]
